You can implement `APIResponse` in your existing method with only a few changes. Since your `studentService.getById(id)` already returns a `StudentResponse`, you don't need to create another `StudentResponse` object.

## Updated controller method

Replace your current method with this:

```

@GetMapping("getById/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<APIResponse<StudentResponse>> getById(
        @PathVariable long id) {

    StudentResponse studentResponse = studentService.getById(id);

    APIResponse<StudentResponse> response =
            APIResponse.success(
                    "Student fetched successfully",
                    studentResponse
            );

    return ResponseEntity.ok(response);
}

```

Add these imports if you don't already have them:

```
import com.example.response.APIResponse;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
```

Keep your existing `StudentResponse` import.

## What changed?

Your original code:

```
public StudentResponse getById(@PathVariable long id) {
    return studentService.getById(id);
}
```

Your updated code follows this flow:

1\. Service

`studentService.getById(id)`

Returns `StudentResponse`

2\. APIResponse

`APIResponse.success(message, studentResponse)`

Wraps the DTO in a common response

3\. ResponseEntity

`ResponseEntity.ok(response)`

Returns HTTP 200 OK

